{
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "C1",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The report does not say whether runs were repeated.",
          "reason": "Repeated testing is neither documented nor explicitly ruled out."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The tested system scored 70% in one reported run.",
          "reason": "One reported score does not establish consistency across runs."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA sections 9.3 and 9.7 are relevant, reinterpreting human retesting as repeated AI evaluation. Repeatability evidence is insufficient, corresponding to 0 rather than demonstrated inadequacy. Section 10 limits interpretation to the stated grid-puzzle use."
    },
    {
      "id": "C2",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "they ran the evaluation exactly once and did not repeat it",
          "reason": "The passage explicitly excludes repeated testing."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "they ran the evaluation exactly once and did not repeat it",
          "reason": "No repeated scores establish stability; this also does not demonstrate instability."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "Under EFPA sections 9.3 and 9.7, repeated-run precision remains unassessable: 0 for insufficient evidence, despite clear evidence that retesting was not conducted. Section 6.2 distinguishes clear reporting from the quality of the evidence reported."
    },
    {
      "id": "C3",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "They also repeated the evaluation ten times with the same system, inputs, instructions, resources, and settings.",
          "reason": "The passage documents repetitions with the stated conditions held constant."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "Scores ranged from 69% to 71%.",
          "reason": "The observed range meets the report's definition of at most two percentage points."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA sections 9.3 and 9.7 support considering these repetitions as precision evidence for this system and setup. The report's stability threshold is not an EFPA rating anchor; ten runs alone do not justify an excellent reliability rating or generalisation to other systems."
    },
    {
      "id": "C4",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "They also repeated the evaluation ten times with the same system, inputs, instructions, resources, and settings.",
          "reason": "Repeated evaluation under the same stated conditions is explicitly documented."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Contradicted",
          "quote": "Scores ranged from 20% to 90%.",
          "reason": "The observed range exceeds the report's stability limit of two percentage points."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA sections 9.3 and 9.7 distinguish conducting a precision study from obtaining satisfactory results. Here, instability is demonstrated against the stated comparison requirement. Under section 9.8, this undermines dependable comparisons for this setup."
    },
    {
      "id": "C5",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "repeat_test",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "The report does not say whether runs were repeated.",
          "reason": "Installation guidance adds no evidence that repeated testing occurred."
        },
        {
          "id": "stable",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "They report no additional evaluation results.",
          "reason": "The added instructions supply no repeated-run findings supporting stability."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "Installation instructions concern EFPA section 6.3.8 on software requirements and support. They do not resolve missing precision evidence under sections 9.3 and 9.7. Documentation and measurement quality require separate judgements."
    },
    {
      "id": "C6",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "narrow",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "System A solves 80 puzzles and system B solves 60 in the reported evaluation.",
          "reason": "A solved more of the evaluated puzzles under the stated scoring and instructions."
        },
        {
          "id": "broad",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Only grid puzzles were tested; no findings about other tasks are supplied.",
          "reason": "The findings do not establish an intelligence difference across domains."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA sections 6.1, 10.1 and 10.5 connect evidence to intended score interpretation. The observed comparison fits the narrow intended use, while broader intelligence claims lack supporting evidence. Section 8.3.2.2 permits norms to be inapplicable for an explicitly ranking-only purpose; it does not authorise broader conclusions."
    },
    {
      "id": "C7",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "narrow",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "System A solves 80 puzzles and system B solves 60 in the reported evaluation.",
          "reason": "The reported results establish the observed advantage on these puzzles."
        },
        {
          "id": "broad",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Only grid puzzles were tested; no findings about other tasks are supplied.",
          "reason": "Stating a broad intended use does not provide evidence supporting it."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA sections 6.1, 10.1 and 10.5 require evidence supporting the intended interpretation. Grid-only findings do not substantiate selection for general intelligence across domains. This evidence gap does not establish that A lacks greater general intelligence."
    },
    {
      "id": "C8",
      "probes": [
        {
          "id": "before",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Initially, only test scores were available.",
          "reason": "The initial scores alone supply no demonstrated relationship with improved workshop decisions."
        },
        {
          "id": "after",
          "label": "Supported",
          "quote": "With A, 90% of decisions were correct; with B, 70%.",
          "reason": "The randomised trial supports higher correctness with A in the studied workshop; the reported difference interval also favours A."
        },
        {
          "id": "safety",
          "label": "Not established",
          "quote": "Time and safety outcomes were not measured.",
          "reason": "Correctness findings do not establish an improvement in an unmeasured safety outcome."
        }
      ],
      "instrument_review": "EFPA sections 10.3.1.6 and 10.3.2 address experimental evidence and relevant external outcomes. Random allocation, new cases, blinded correctness checks and the uncertainty interval strengthen support for the stated workshop use. Section 10.5 limits conclusions to decision correctness in these conditions. The 120 inspectors do not constitute 120 AI systems or establish benchmark reliability under section 9."
    }
  ],
  "closing_note": "No task instructions were unclear. The requested probe format permits relevant-section reviews rather than complete forms. Throughout, AI systems replace human test takers and repeated evaluations replace human retesting where appropriate; literal human disability, demographic, qualification and debriefing questions are inapplicable to AI subjects. Human-facing usability and stakeholder fairness may still apply, but evidence is insufficient here. Unreported norms, materials or generated reports are not assumed defective or absent. Probe labels are not EFPA ratings, and no aggregate quality score or EFPA endorsement is implied. Instrument source: EFPA Test Review Model 2025, version 5.0, supplied extraction, CC BY-SA 4.0, https://www.efpa.eu/."
}
